# A field declared by VALUE carries a DEFAULT, and a default is a constant
# numeric expression: `defaultval.literal` holds a node the emitter re-emits
# verbatim (spec.pdoc: "a constant numeric expression (for fields and parameters
# with default values)"). A String cannot be one -- it needs a runtime
# allocation -- so a string literal in declaration position names no type and
# leaves the field with none.
#
# It was ACCEPTED. Beside a field that did resolve, zc reported nothing at all
# and emitted C naming an undeclared struct, with `Z_?_TAG_` written into the
# output where the unresolved type leaked through verbatim. Alone it surfaced
# as "missing required arguments 'tag' (type: ?)" against whoever later tried
# to CONSTRUCT the type -- neither the mistake nor the place it was made.
#
# The refusal names the field and points at the declaration. A real String
# field (`s: String`, filled by the constructor) is unaffected, and so is a
# numeric default and a variant-arm one.
Counter: class {
    pos: 0
    tag: "counter".string
}

main: function is {
    c: Counter
    print "pos=\{c.pos}"
}
